          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong pseudofunctor
            is a specific algebraic notion of
            %em
              weak
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functor"} 2-functor
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategories
            (including
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functor"} 2-functor
            which preserves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identities
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-morphisms"} 1-morphisms
            only up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent"} coherent
            specified 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"}> isomorphism
            \. (In contrast to
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-functors"} strict 2-functors
          %p
            In general, there is not much reason to say “pseudofunctor” instead of “functor,” since by far the most important type of functor between arbitrary bicategories is weak. However, if the domain and codomain are known to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-categories
            (including ordinary
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-functor"} strict 2-functor
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p
            Pseudo or weak functors are also to be distinguished from
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+functor"} lax functor
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+functor"} oplax functor
          %p An older terminology, which should probably be avoided at all costs, uses “homomorphism of bicategories” for a weak functor and “morphism of bicategories” for a lax one.
